ACADEMY.reg("bank_en", [{"id":"CQE-41","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>In the Cost of Quality model, the cost of CALIBRATING instruments and of INSPECTING incoming material is classified as:</p>","opts":["<p>Appraisal cost</p>","<p>Internal failure</p>","<p>External failure</p>","<p>Prevention cost</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Appraisal is the cost of VERIFYING conformance: inspection, testing, product audit, calibration. Prevention is the cost of AVOIDING nonconformance: training, planning, design review, supplier capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Internal failure is the cost of nonconformance detected before delivery: scrap, rework, reinspection.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — External failure is the cost after delivery to the customer: warranty, recall, return, loss of reputation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Prevention acts before there's any product to appraise. Training the inspector is prevention; the inspection they perform is appraisal.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 58</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Metrology","chapterRef":"cap-058","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-42","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>The essential responsibility of a process owner is:</p>","opts":["<p>Performing the process tasks on a daily basis.</p>","<p>Having authority and accountability for the end-to-end performance of the process, across functional boundaries.</p>","<p>Approving the budget of the department where the process is located.</p>","<p>Auditing the process independently.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Real processes cross departments; the org chart doesn't. Without someone accountable for the whole flow, each function optimizes its own piece and the overall result degrades. Authority without end-to-end accountability isn't 'process ownership.'</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Performing the work is the role of the process operators; the owner is accountable for performance, not for execution.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Budget authority over a department is exactly the functional view that process ownership exists to correct.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Auditing requires INDEPENDENCE from whoever is accountable for the process — the owner can't audit their own process.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 47</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Non-Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-047","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-43","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>In Tuckman's sequence, the 'storming' phase of a project team is characterized by:</p>","opts":["<p>Initial politeness and dependence on the facilitator.</p>","<p>High productivity and stabilized roles.</p>","<p>Conflict over roles, approach, and leadership, being a normal and necessary phase.</p>","<p>Dissolution of the team after delivery.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Forming → Storming → Norming → Performing → Adjourning. Storming is normal and productive when facilitated: it's where the real disagreements surface. Teams where it never happens are frequently not aligned — they're just quiet.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That's 'forming,' the initial phase.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's 'performing,' the mature phase.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That's 'adjourning,' the closure.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-44","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A quality engineer is pressured to approve a marginal lot, on the grounds that the customer won't notice. Under the ASQ Code of Ethics, the correct conduct is:</p>","opts":["<p>Approve it under verbal authorization from their manager.</p>","<p>Hand the decision off to another colleague.</p>","<p>Approve it, documenting the pressure received in an internal note.</p>","<p>Refuse the approval and escalate through the defined channels, keeping an objective record of the data and the decision.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The code places safety and the public interest above the convenience of the employer or the customer, and requires the professional to issue only true and objective statements. Documented escalation protects the public, the organization, and the professional themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Verbal authorization doesn't change the technical reality or transfer professional responsibility.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Passing the decision along is evasion; the dilemma remains and the product ships regardless.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Documenting the pressure doesn't make an improper approval acceptable; the nonconformance still stands.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 13</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ASQ Code of Ethics for Professional Conduct","chapterRef":"cap-013","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-45","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>After an SPC training session, the organization wants to assess its effectiveness. Which evidence is STRONGEST, in terms of Kirkpatrick's levels?</p>","opts":["<p>A sustained reduction in uninvestigated control-chart alarms, observed over the following weeks.</p>","<p>The percentage of workers who attended the training.</p>","<p>A participant satisfaction survey at the end of the session.</p>","<p>The result of the theory test given at the end of the training.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Kirkpatrick: level 1 reaction, 2 learning, 3 behavior, 4 results. Evidence of changed behavior at the workstation and in the process indicator is level 3/4 — the only kind that demonstrates real effectiveness. ISO 9001 asks for effectiveness, not for training having taken place.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Attendance is an indicator that the training happened, not that it was effective — it's the most common mistake on this clause.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Satisfaction is level 1: it measures how participants felt, not what they started doing differently.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The test is level 2: it shows knowledge was acquired, which often doesn't translate into practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 15</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Training","chapterRef":"cap-015","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-46","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>'Catchball' in Hoshin Kanri refers to:</p>","opts":["<p>The annual audit of goal attainment.</p>","<p>The iterative, vertical and horizontal dialogue that negotiates goals and means between levels, before commitment.</p>","<p>The brainstorming technique for generating annual objectives.</p>","<p>The visual board where indicators are posted.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Catchball is the back-and-forth negotiation between levels: leadership proposes the what, operational levels respond with the how and the feasibility, and the objectives get adjusted. It's what distinguishes Hoshin from an imposed cascade of targets — and it's usually the part that's skipped when Hoshin fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Review is a different step in the annual cycle (including the president's review).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Brainstorming may occur, but catchball is specifically the vertical and horizontal negotiation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The visual board is a tracking tool, not the negotiation process.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 29</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hoshin Kanri: Policy Deployment in Lean Management","chapterRef":"cap-029","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-47","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>The High Level Structure (Annex SL / Harmonized Structure) exists to:</p>","opts":["<p>Replace sector-specific requirements.</p>","<p>Force every organization to certify to multiple standards.</p>","<p>Give management-system standards a common clause structure and text, making integration easier.</p>","<p>Define product technical requirements.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Common clauses 4 through 10, common definitions, and common core text let ISO 9001, 14001, 45001, 27001, and 42001 share context, leadership, planning, support, operation, evaluation, and improvement — reducing duplication in integrated systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Sector requirements (IATF, AS9100, ISO 13485) are added on top of the common structure, not replaced by it.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It imposes no certification at all; it's a convention for how standards are written.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Product requirements come from product standards or customer specifications, not from Annex SL.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 90</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Standards and Other Guidelines: The ISO 9000 Series and the Malcolm Baldrige Award","chapterRef":"cap-090","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-48","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>The central purpose of document control is to ensure that:</p>","opts":["<p>Only management has access to procedures.</p>","<p>All documents are kept as signed paper copies.</p>","<p>All documents are archived indefinitely.</p>","<p>The correct, approved version is available at the point of use, and obsolete versions aren't used by mistake.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Both halves matter equally. Missing the right version where it's used → work done off-standard. An accessible obsolete version → work done to the wrong standard, made worse by there being 'documented evidence' backing it up. It's a recurring audit finding.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Restricting access from whoever does the work prevents correct use of the document.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The medium doesn't matter; what matters is version control, approval, and distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Indefinite retention is unnecessary and counterproductive; retention periods are set by legal, contractual, and risk requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 89</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality System Documentation","chapterRef":"cap-089","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-49","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>An audit performed by the organization on its own management system, by trained internal auditors, is classified as:</p>","opts":["<p>First-party audit</p>","<p>Second-party audit</p>","<p>Third-party audit</p>","<p>Certification audit</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>First party = internal (the organization audits itself). Second party = the organization audits a supplier, or a customer audits it. Third party = an independent body, typically for certification.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Second party involves an interested party with a contractual relationship — customer or supplier.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Third party requires a body external to and independent of both parties.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Certification is a specific case of a third-party audit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 93</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Audits","chapterRef":"cap-093","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-50","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>What's the difference between CORRECTION and CORRECTIVE ACTION?</p>","opts":["<p>They're synonyms in ISO terminology.</p>","<p>Correction eliminates the detected nonconformity; corrective action eliminates the CAUSE to prevent recurrence.</p>","<p>Correction is done by quality and corrective action by production.</p>","<p>Correction applies to product and corrective action to documents.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Reworking the part is correction. Finding out why the process produced it that way and changing the process is corrective action. Systems where 100% of 'corrective actions' are rework and reinspection have no corrective action at all — and the nonconformity keeps recurring.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — ISO 9000 defines the two terms separately, and the distinction is one of the most heavily tested.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The distinction is about the nature of the action, not who performs it.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Both apply to any type of nonconformity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 93</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Audits","chapterRef":"cap-093","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-51","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>Under ISO 9001, management review has as a mandatory output:</p>","opts":["<p>The updated list of approved suppliers.</p>","<p>Minutes signed by every participant.</p>","<p>Decisions and actions on improvement opportunities, the need for changes to the system, and resource needs.</p>","<p>The annual financial report.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The standard specifies inputs (performance, audits, customer feedback, risks and opportunities, resource adequacy) and OUTPUTS in the form of decisions and actions. A review that only presents indicators without producing decisions doesn't meet the requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Supplier management is an operational process, covered under the external-provision-control clause.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Minutes are evidence of the process, not the substantive output required.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Financial information can be a context input, but it isn't an output of the management review.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 87</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Quality Management System (QMS)","chapterRef":"cap-087","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-52","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>In ISO 19011, the 'evidence-based approach' principle means that:</p>","opts":["<p>The entire population of records must be examined.</p>","<p>Evidence must be collected only from documents, not by observation.</p>","<p>The auditor should rely on the auditee's verbal statement when the auditee is experienced.</p>","<p>Audit conclusions must rest on verifiable evidence, obtained through appropriate sampling of the information available.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>An audit operates under a time constraint, so it works by sampling — which introduces sampling risk that the auditor must manage consciously. Conclusions must be traceable to verifiable evidence, not to impressions or personal trust.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 100% examination is impractical and isn't required; the standard explicitly acknowledges sampling.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Direct observation, interview, and measurement are legitimate sources, often more revealing than the document.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Statements are a type of information to be corroborated, not self-sufficient evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 92</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 19011:2026 — Management System Auditing","chapterRef":"cap-092","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-24","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>The essential difference between DFMEA and PFMEA lies in:</p>","opts":["<p>DFMEA is done by quality and PFMEA by production.</p>","<p>DFMEA uses a 1-10 scale and PFMEA uses a 1-5 scale.</p>","<p>DFMEA analyzes failure modes attributable to the product's DESIGN; PFMEA analyzes failure modes generated by the manufacturing PROCESS.</p>","<p>DFMEA is mandatory under IATF 16949 and PFMEA is optional.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The distinction is the object of analysis, and it determines what can and can't be assumed. PFMEA takes the design as a given and asks what the process can do wrong; DFMEA questions the design itself. A design failure isn't fixed with process control.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Both are required to be cross-functional. Assigning either to a single department is one of the most common causes of low-quality FMEAs.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both use 1-to-10 scales under the AIAG-VDA reference. It's the criteria tables that differ.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Both are required when applicable; IATF requires both design and process risk analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 82</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA Types in Practice — DFMEA, PFMEA, UFMEA, and the Link to the Control Plan","chapterRef":"cap-082","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-25","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>Design verification and design validation differ because:</p>","opts":["<p>Verification is done by the supplier and validation by the customer.</p>","<p>Verification is document-based and validation is always physical testing.</p>","<p>Verification happens after production and validation before.</p>","<p>Verification confirms the product meets the specified requirements; validation confirms it meets the actual need in use.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The classic formulation: verification asks 'did we build the product RIGHT?' (against the specification); validation asks 'did we build the RIGHT product?' (against the need). It's possible to pass 100% of verification and fail validation — the specification was wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Who performs it doesn't define the nature of the activity. Validation can be performed by the supplier under simulated conditions of use.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both can involve analysis, testing, inspection, or demonstration; the distinction is the reference, not the method.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The sequence is reversed and irrelevant: verification precedes validation in the development flow.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 97</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Verification and Validation","chapterRef":"cap-097","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-26","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In GD&amp;T, the datum's purpose is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Establish the theoretical origin from which geometric relationships are measured, ensuring repeatability of the inspection setup.</p>","<p>Define the part's surface finish.</p>","<p>Specify the part's material.</p>","<p>Indicate the drawing's tightest tolerance.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Without a datum, two people measure the same part two different ways and get different results — both 'correct.' The datum reference frame (primary, secondary, tertiary) fixes the six degrees of freedom and makes measurement reproducible, which ties directly into MSA.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Surface finish has its own symbology, independent of datums.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Material is specified in the title block or a note, not through the datum system.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A datum isn't a tolerance; it's a geometric reference.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 96</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Technical Drawings and Specifications","chapterRef":"cap-096","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-27","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In a 4-component chain with independent, symmetric tolerances, RSS (root sum of squares) stacking, compared with the worst-case method:</p>","opts":["<p>Always gives a larger result, making it more conservative.</p>","<p>Gives a smaller result, accepting a small but nonzero probability that the assembly exceeds the tolerance.</p>","<p>Gives exactly the same result when the tolerances are equal.</p>","<p>Is only valid when the components are made on the same machine.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Worst case sums the tolerances (T = ΣTᵢ) and assumes every component is simultaneously at its extreme — an extremely unlikely event. RSS uses T = √(ΣTᵢ²), which is always smaller, and accepts a residual fraction of out-of-tolerance assemblies. It's the right choice in high-volume production, and the wrong choice for safety-critical parts made in small quantities.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's worst case that gives the larger, more conservative result; RSS is always smaller or equal.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — With n equal tolerances T: worst case = nT, RSS = T√n. They only coincide for n=1.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — RSS requires statistical INDEPENDENCE. Manufacturing on the same machine tends to create correlation, which INVALIDATES RSS — exactly the opposite.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 140</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Uncertainty Quantification and Monte Carlo for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-140","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-28","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>Three components with reliabilities 0.95, 0.90, and 0.98 operate in SERIES. What is the system's reliability?</p>","opts":["<p>0.98</p>","<p>0.943</p>","<p>0.838</p>","<p>0.999</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In series, all must work: R = 0.95 × 0.90 × 0.98 ≈ 0.8379. The engineering lesson is that series reliability is always LOWER than the worst component — adding elements in series always degrades the system.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.98 is the best component's reliability; in series the system never exceeds the worst one, let alone the best.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 0.943 is the arithmetic mean of the three, which has no meaning in reliability.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 0.999 would correspond to a parallel (redundant) configuration, where R = 1−∏(1−Rᵢ).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 98</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Maintainability","chapterRef":"cap-098","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-29","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A supplier states an MTBF of 100,000 hours for a sensor. The correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The guaranteed service life is 100,000 hours.</p>","<p>Each sensor will last approximately 100,000 hours before failing.</p>","<p>Under a constant failure rate, the rate is 1/100,000 per hour; reliability at 100,000 h is only e⁻¹ ≈ 37%.</p>","<p>No sensor will fail before 100,000 hours.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>MTBF is the MEAN of an exponential distribution, not a guaranteed life. At t = MTBF, R(t) = e^(−t/MTBF) = e⁻¹ ≈ 0.368 — meaning about 63% of units have already failed. Confusing MTBF with service life is one of the most costly mistakes in maintenance planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — MTBF is a population statistic, not a contractual commitment about any individual unit's life.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It's the mean, not the typical value. For an exponential, the median is ≈0.693×MTBF, well below the mean.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Many units fail well before that: the exponential distribution has no lower bound on life.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 98</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Maintainability","chapterRef":"cap-098","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-30","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>The main purpose of QFD's 'house of quality' is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Document the process failure modes and their controls.</p>","<p>Replace capability analysis during the design phase.</p>","<p>Prioritize Six Sigma projects by financial return.</p>","<p>Relate customer requirements (the what) to technical characteristics (the how), including correlations between them and competitive comparison.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The matrix crosses VOC with measurable technical characteristics, weighs their importance, assesses the competition, and — in the 'roof' — exposes correlations between technical characteristics, revealing where improving one degrades another. That's where QFD pays off the most, and it's the part most often skipped.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That's FMEA. QFD is a requirements-translation tool, not a failure-analysis one.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They're complementary: QFD defines what to measure, capability assesses whether the process can deliver it.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Project prioritization uses selection matrices and business cases, not the house of quality.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 114</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma in Finance","chapterRef":"cap-114","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-31","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A 2⁷⁻⁴ fractional factorial design has resolution III. What is the practical consequence?</p>","opts":["<p>Main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.</p>","<p>Main effects are clear, but two-factor interactions are confounded with each other.</p>","<p>There's no confounding; the design estimates all effects.</p>","<p>The design requires mandatory replicates to be valid.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Resolution refers to the length of the shortest word in the defining relation. Resolution III: main effects confounded with 2-factor interactions. It's used for SCREENING (identifying the few active factors among many), never for optimization or a final conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That describes resolution IV: main effects clear of 2-factor interactions, but those interactions confounded with each other.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — No confounding at all is the full factorial (2⁷ = 128 runs); the 2⁷⁻⁴ fraction uses only 8.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Replicates improve the error estimate but don't remove the structural confounding, which is set by the generators.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 75</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Fractional Factorials: Resolution, Generators, and Alias Structure","chapterRef":"cap-075","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-13","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Lots of varying size are inspected, and the NUMBER OF DEFECTS per lot is recorded (a single unit can have several defects). Which control chart is appropriate?</p>","opts":["<p>X̄-R chart</p>","<p>p chart</p>","<p>np chart</p>","<p>u chart</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Defects (not defective units) follow a Poisson model: c or u charts. Since the sample size varies, you need to normalize per unit of opportunity — the u chart, with limits that vary at each point.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — X̄-R is for continuous measured-variable data, not counts.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The p chart is for the PROPORTION of defective units (binomial), not a count of defects. A unit with 3 defects counts as 1 defective on a p chart — information is lost.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The np chart is for the NUMBER of defective units and requires a constant sample size — both conditions fail here.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-14","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>The rational subgroup principle requires that:</p>","opts":["<p>The variation WITHIN a subgroup reflect only common causes, and special causes show up BETWEEN subgroups.</p>","<p>Each subgroup contain parts from every machine, to represent the overall process.</p>","<p>The subgroup size always be 5, by Shewhart's convention.</p>","<p>Subgroup parts be selected at random across the entire shift.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Shewhart designed the chart so that σ_within serves as the 'ruler' for background noise. If special causes get into the subgroup, they inflate the ruler, the limits widen, and the chart stops detecting what it's supposed to detect.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Mixing machines within a subgroup is the classic pathology: between-machine variation gets folded into σ_within, the limits end up wide, and the chart never alarms. Each machine should have its own chart.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — n=5 is common for historical convenience, not principle. Size should follow from the shift you want to detect and the process logic.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Sampling at random across the whole shift deliberately mixes different conditions — it's exactly the opposite of a rational subgroup (it's 'stratified sampling' and produces the second pathology).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 66</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Rational Subgrouping: the Decision That Determines Everything That Follows","chapterRef":"cap-066","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-15","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A Gage R&amp;R study by ANOVA returns %GRR = 12% (over tolerance) and ndc = 3. What is the correct conclusion?</p>","opts":["<p>System accepted without reservation: %GRR below 30% is enough.</p>","<p>System marginally acceptable for tolerance-based control, but with insufficient resolution to distinguish process categories.</p>","<p>System rejected, because %GRR above 10% is always unacceptable.</p>","<p>The two indicators are redundant; reporting %GRR alone is enough.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The two indicators answer different questions. %GRR = 12% is acceptable relative to TOLERANCE (10–30% zone, acceptable depending on application and cost). But ndc = 3 (&lt; 5) indicates the system barely distinguishes 3 categories within the PROCESS variation — insufficient for using the measurements in SPC, capability, or improvement work.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ignoring ndc is the most common mistake in reading an MSA. A system can pass on tolerance and still be useless for studying the process.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The AIAG criterion isn't a rigid 10% cutoff: 10–30% is the conditional zone, decided by the characteristic's criticality and the cost of measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — They aren't redundant: %GRR compares against tolerance (or total variation), and ndc compares against part-to-part variation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-16","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A caliper measures 10 mm parts correctly but shows a growing deviation as it approaches 200 mm. Which metrology property is compromised?</p>","opts":["<p>Repeatability</p>","<p>Stability</p>","<p>Linearity</p>","<p>Reproducibility</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Linearity is how BIAS behaves across the measurement RANGE. Bias that changes with the measured magnitude is, by definition, a lack of linearity — typically corrected with multi-point calibration instead of a single point.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Repeatability is the spread of the same operator repeatedly measuring the same part — variation, not a magnitude-dependent systematic shift.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Stability is bias drift over TIME, with the same reference part. Here what varies is the measured dimension, not time.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Reproducibility is variation BETWEEN operators. The scenario doesn't involve different operators.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 60</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Accuracy: Bias, Linearity, and Stability","chapterRef":"cap-060","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-17","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>What is the technically correct interpretation of an AQL of 1.0% in an ISO 2859-1 plan?</p>","opts":["<p>The maximum percentage of defective units the customer accepts receiving in any given lot.</p>","<p>The percentage of defectives that corresponds to a 50% probability of acceptance.</p>","<p>No lot with more than 1.0% nonconforming units will be accepted.</p>","<p>The process quality level that, if maintained, results in a high probability of lot acceptance across the series.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>AQL is a property of the SERIES of lots and of the supplier's process, not a per-lot guarantee. It's the quality level treated as satisfactory, associated with high Pa (typically 0.89–0.99 depending on the plan). Real per-lot protection is read off the full OC curve, not the AQL label.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — This is the most widespread commercial misunderstanding about AQL, and the standard itself warns against it. AQL isn't a per-lot contractual limit — that's what ISO 2859-2 (LQ) is for.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The point where Pa = 50% is the point of indifference, not the AQL.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — False: the OC curve is continuous. A lot with 3% nonconforming still has a non-negligible probability of being accepted by a plan with AQL 1.0%.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 63</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Acceptance Sampling: Fundamental Concepts and the Operating Characteristic Curve","chapterRef":"cap-063","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-18","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Under normal inspection in ISO 2859-1, the switching rules trigger a move to TIGHTENED inspection when:</p>","opts":["<p>2 out of 5 consecutive lots are rejected under original inspection.</p>","<p>Production is halted for more than 3 months.</p>","<p>The supplier requests it, for contractual reasons.</p>","<p>A single lot is rejected.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The rule is 2 rejections out of 5 consecutive lots (original inspection). The switching rules are what give the standard its real protective power over time: without them, the AQL scheme loses much of its effectiveness — it's the part most often ignored in practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A prolonged halt affects eligibility for reduced inspection and series continuity, it doesn't trigger tightened inspection.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The switching rules are mandatory and evidence-based, not negotiable for the parties' convenience.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A single, isolated rejection doesn't trigger switching; the scheme tolerates random fluctuation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 64</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sampling Standards and Plans: ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 and Z1.9","chapterRef":"cap-064","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-19","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Which element is indispensable in a Control Plan and is frequently omitted?</p>","opts":["<p>The unit cost of the controlled characteristic.</p>","<p>The reaction plan: what to do, who decides, and how to handle suspect product when the control signals.</p>","<p>The commercial name of the measurement equipment.</p>","<p>The complete revision history of the product drawing.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>A control that detects but doesn't define the reaction controls nothing. The reaction plan has to answer three questions: immediate action on the process, disposition of material produced since the last conforming check, and who has authority to decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cost is an input to characteristic selection and cost-benefit analysis, not a control-plan field.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The measurement system's method and capability matter; the brand name doesn't.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The control plan references the currently effective revision level; the full history belongs to document control.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 65</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Product and Process Control Methods","chapterRef":"cap-065","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-20","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>An X̄ chart shows 9 consecutive points on the same side of the centerline, all within the control limits. The correct action is:</p>","opts":["<p>None: since every point is within the limits, the process is in control.</p>","<p>Recalculate the limits using the recent data, to re-center them on the new mean.</p>","<p>Investigate: the pattern is a special-cause signal under the detection rules, indicating a shift in the mean.</p>","<p>Increase the subgroup size to reduce the chart's sensitivity.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The rule of 9 (or 8, depending on the Nelson/Western Electric rule set adopted) exists because the probability of 9 points in a row on the same side by chance is tiny (≈2×(0.5)⁹ ≈ 0.4%). It's a legitimate signal of a sustained shift in the mean, even without a 3σ limit violation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Restricting the analysis to the 3σ limits wastes the information in the run patterns, which detect small, persistent shifts much earlier.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Recalculating limits to accommodate an uninvestigated shift is 'normalizing the deviance': it institutionalizes the change instead of finding the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Increasing n INCREASES the X̄ chart's sensitivity (the standard error decreases). Deliberately reducing sensitivity to stop seeing the signal is the opposite of the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-21","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>In an ATTRIBUTE MSA study (pass/fail) against a known reference standard, which metric assesses agreement beyond chance?</p>","opts":["<p>Pearson correlation coefficient</p>","<p>%GRR by the range method</p>","<p>Cpk index of the measurement system</p>","<p>Cohen's kappa index</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>With nominal data, raw agreement rate is misleading: two evaluators who pass everything agree 100% of the time without discriminating anything. Kappa corrects for agreement expected by chance (usual practical criterion: κ ≥ 0.75 good; &lt; 0.40 unacceptable).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Pearson requires continuous data and measures linear association; it doesn't apply to binary classifications.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — %GRR by the range method is for variables data; there's no meaningful range between 'pass' and 'fail.'</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Cpk is a process-capability index against specifications; it doesn't assess attribute measurement systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-22","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Furnace temperature data, collected every minute, shows strong autocorrelation (lag-1 ACF ≈ 0.85). Applying a conventional I-MR chart produces:</p>","opts":["<p>Limits that are too narrow and excess false alarms.</p>","<p>Correct results, because the I chart doesn't assume independence.</p>","<p>An inability to calculate the moving range.</p>","<p>Limits that are too wide and an almost total absence of alarms.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The moving range between strongly correlated adjacent observations is artificially SMALL (neighboring points look alike). This underestimates σ, narrows the limits, and generates constant alarms over variation that's just the process's natural behavior — the operator learns to ignore the chart. The fix is to model the series (e.g., ARIMA) and chart the RESIDUALS, or resample at sufficiently spaced intervals.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Every Shewhart chart assumes independence; the I chart is the most sensitive to violating it, precisely because it uses consecutive observations.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The moving range is always calculable — the problem is that the calculated value stops estimating what it's supposed to.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That's the opposite effect. Wide limits occur when special causes enter the σ estimator, not with positive autocorrelation measured via moving range.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 70</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Short-Run SPC, Autocorrelation, and Rare Events","chapterRef":"cap-070","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-23","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>An inspector always collects samples from the boxes on top of the pallet, because they're accessible. What problem does this introduce?</p>","opts":["<p>Increased Type I error, with no systematic bias.</p>","<p>Selection bias: the sample no longer represents the lot, and the calculated confidence interval is invalid.</p>","<p>Just a loss of statistical efficiency, fixable by increasing n.</p>","<p>A normality violation, fixable by transformation.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Convenience sampling breaks the assumption that underlies all inference: that the sample represents the lot. If the top boxes are systematically different (most recent production, less compression, different handling), the estimate is biased, and no downstream statistical treatment fixes that.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Bias isn't Type I error. Type I error is a false-positive rate under valid sampling; here validity itself has collapsed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — This is the central conceptual mistake: increasing n with a biased method gives a WRONG estimate with more precision. The bias doesn't converge to zero.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The problem is with the sampling design, not the shape of the distribution; no transformation restores representativeness.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 62</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Integrity: Random Sampling, Selection Bias, and Chain of Custody","chapterRef":"cap-062","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-32","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>The exit gate for DMAIC's MEASURE phase should require, at minimum:</p>","opts":["<p>The solution already selected and the pilot planned.</p>","<p>An operational definition of the metrics, a validated measurement system (MSA), and a data-established baseline.</p>","<p>The project charter signed by the sponsor.</p>","<p>The final control plan and handoff to the process owner.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Without an operational definition, two people measure different things under the same name. Without MSA, you don't know whether the observed variation belongs to the process or the gauge. Without a baseline, there'll be no way to demonstrate improvement in Control. These three items are what Measure has to deliver.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Selecting a solution in Measure is the most common mistake in DMAIC projects: it skips root-cause analysis and solves the wrong problem.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The charter is a DEFINE deliverable, not a Measure one.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Control plan and handoff are CONTROL deliverables.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 33</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Introduction to DMAIC and DMADV","chapterRef":"cap-033","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-33","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A defect Pareto shows 'other' as the largest bar, at 40% of occurrences. The appropriate action is:</p>","opts":["<p>Conclude the defects are random and there's no priority.</p>","<p>Attack the 'other' category, since it's the largest.</p>","<p>Review the categorization and break 'other' down into identifiable causes before prioritizing.</p>","<p>Remove 'other' from the chart to clarify the analysis.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>A dominant residual category signals a failure in data collection, not a discovery. You can't act on 'other.' You need to go back to the collection point, understand what's in there, and create actionable categories — that's often where the real problem lives.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The apparent spread is an artifact of poor categorization, not evidence of randomness.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 'Other' isn't a cause; it's the absence of an identified cause. There's no countermeasure for a residual category.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Removing 'other' hides 40% of the data and distorts the cumulative percentages of the remaining categories.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 8</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma Basic Concepts","chapterRef":"cap-008","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-34","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>The '5 Whys' are inadequate as the sole root-cause tool when:</p>","opts":["<p>There's a documented record of the event.</p>","<p>The failure is simple and has a linear causal chain.</p>","<p>The team has experience with the process.</p>","<p>The failure results from multiple interacting causes, or is intermittent and condition-dependent.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The 5 Whys assume a single, linear causal chain. Multifactor, intermittent, or interaction-dependent failures require tools that represent simultaneity and branching — fault tree, DOE, or statistical analysis. Applying the 5 Whys to a multifactor problem produces a plausible and false narrative.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Good records improve any root-cause analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's exactly the situation where the 5 Whys work well.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Experience helps; the limitation is structural to the tool, not the group.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 90</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Standards and Other Guidelines: The ISO 9000 Series and the Malcolm Baldrige Award","chapterRef":"cap-090","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-35","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A line has a WIP of 240 units and an output rate of 30 units/hour. By Little's Law, what is the lead time?</p>","opts":["<p>8 hours</p>","<p>0.125 hour</p>","<p>7200 hours</p>","<p>270 hours</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Little's Law: LT = WIP / output rate = 240/30 = 8 hours. It's the most underrated relationship in operations management: reducing WIP reduces lead time proportionally, with no investment in capacity at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 0.125 h = 30/240 — the ratio is inverted.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 7200 h = 240 × 30 — multiplication instead of division.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 270 = 240 + 30, a sum with no dimensional meaning.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 20</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Takt Time, Cycle Time, Lead Time, and Little's Law","chapterRef":"cap-020","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-36","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A piece of equipment has Availability = 0.90, Performance = 0.95, and Quality = 0.99. What is the OEE?</p>","opts":["<p>0.947</p>","<p>0.846</p>","<p>0.99</p>","<p>0.90</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>OEE = 0.90 × 0.95 × 0.99 ≈ 0.846 (84.6%). The multiplicative nature is the message: three 'good' factors produce a result substantially lower than any one of them. Reporting only availability hides the real loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.947 is the average of the three factors — OEE is a product, not an average.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 0.99 is just the quality factor.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 0.90 is just availability — precisely the mistake of reporting one factor as if it were the OEE.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 25</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"TPM and OEE: Total Productive Maintenance","chapterRef":"cap-025","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-37","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In SMED, the fundamental distinction that enables changeover-time reduction is between:</p>","opts":["<p>Internal activities (only possible with the machine stopped) and external ones (possible while the machine is running).</p>","<p>Operator tasks and maintenance-technician tasks.</p>","<p>First-shift setup and subsequent-shift setup.</p>","<p>Manual work and automated work.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>SMED's biggest gain, and its cheapest one, comes from identifying activities classified as internal out of habit (fetching a tool, preheating a mold, checking a document) and converting them to external. Only after that do you technically optimize what's left that's genuinely internal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Who performs it is secondary to when the activity can be performed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The shift is irrelevant to classifying the activities.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Automating is one possible technique, usually expensive, and not the structuring distinction.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 24</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"SMED: Single-Minute Exchange of Die","chapterRef":"cap-024","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-38","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A PREVENTION poka-yoke is distinguished from a DETECTION one because it:</p>","opts":["<p>Is cheaper to implement.</p>","<p>Makes the error physically impossible, instead of signaling the error after it's made.</p>","<p>Requires electronic sensors.</p>","<p>Is applied only at final inspection.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Prevention (control) stops the occurrence: an asymmetric connector can't be plugged in backwards. Detection (warning) alerts after the fact and depends on the operator reacting. The hierarchy is always: eliminate &gt; prevent &gt; detect &gt; warn.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's often cheaper (a pin, a chamfer), but cost isn't the defining characteristic.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The most robust poka-yokes tend to be mechanical and passive, with no electronics.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A poka-yoke should sit as close as possible to the source of the error; by final inspection the waste has already happened.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 26</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Jidoka and Poka-Yoke: Quality Built Into the Process","chapterRef":"cap-026","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-39","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A process is out of statistical control (special causes present) and the calculated Cpk is 1.45. The correct priority is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Recalculate Cpk excluding the out-of-control points.</p>","<p>Accept the capability, because Cpk &gt; 1.33.</p>","<p>Eliminate the special causes first; in an unstable process, Cpk isn't a valid forecast.</p>","<p>Increase final-inspection frequency and leave the process as is.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Capability is a FORECAST about future production, and a forecast requires a predictable process. In an unstable process, Cpk describes a past that won't repeat itself. Stability first, capability after — that's the rule that structures all of PART VII.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Excluding points with no identified and corrected assignable cause is dressing up the index; if the cause was identified and eliminated, then yes, collect a fresh data set.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The Cpk&gt;1.33 criterion assumes a stable process. Without stability, the number doesn't mean what it appears to.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Inspecting more is managing the symptom at permanent cost, without touching the cause.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 41</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Beyond Generic DMAIC: the Four Continuous Improvement Paths","chapterRef":"cap-041","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-40","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>Parts wait 3 days between machining and painting because painting processes in large batches. Which waste BEST describes the root cause?</p>","opts":["<p>Correction</p>","<p>Talent</p>","<p>Motion</p>","<p>Overproduction, which generates inventory and waiting</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Producing in batches larger than what the next step needs is overproduction — considered the root waste because it GENERATES the others: the excess becomes inventory, the inventory becomes waiting, and both hide quality problems by delaying detection.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Correction is rework due to a defect; there's no defect in the scenario.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Talent is underutilization of people — a real waste, but not the cause of this flow.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Motion is unnecessary movement of people; it's not what the scenario describes.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 18</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean Concepts","chapterRef":"cap-018","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-01","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A stable process has a mean of 50.0 and a standard deviation (within subgroups) of 2.0. The specification limits are LSL = 44.0 and USL = 56.0. What is the Cpk?</p>","opts":["<p>0.50</p>","<p>1.00</p>","<p>1.50</p>","<p>2.00</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Cpk = min[(USL−μ)/3σ, (μ−LSL)/3σ] = min[(56−50)/6, (50−44)/6] = min[1.00; 1.00] = 1.00. Since the process is centered, Cpk = Cp = 1.00.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.50 would result from using 6σ in the denominator instead of 3σ — a common formula mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 1.50 would correspond to σ = 1.33; the scenario gives σ = 2.0.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 2.00 is the Cp value if you used the full range (USL−LSL)/3σ, forgetting to divide by 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-02","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A PPAP report reads: Cp = 1.80 and Cpk = 0.90. What is the correct engineering reading?</p>","opts":["<p>The data violates normality and the indices can't be calculated.</p>","<p>There was a calculation error, because Cpk can never be half of Cp.</p>","<p>The process is incapable and the variation needs to be reduced before any other action.</p>","<p>The process has small enough variation, but it's off-center relative to the target.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Cp measures only potential (spec width vs. process width) and ignores location. Cpk penalizes off-centering. High Cp with low Cpk is the classic signature of a process with good spread but off-center — usually fixed with a setup adjustment, far cheaper than reducing variation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Nothing in the scenario indicates non-normality; and the Cp≫Cpk pattern has a simpler, more direct explanation.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Cpk ≤ Cp always, and Cpk = Cp/2 is perfectly possible when the mean is shifted.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Reducing variation would be expensive and unnecessary: Cp = 1.80 shows the variation is already adequate.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-03","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A two-sided t-test returns p = 0.03 with α = 0.05. Which statement is correct?</p>","opts":["<p>If H0 were true, data as extreme as or more extreme than what was observed would occur in 3% of repetitions of the study.</p>","<p>The observed effect is large enough to justify changing the process.</p>","<p>The probability that the null hypothesis is true is 3%.</p>","<p>The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true is 97%.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The p-value is a conditional probability that runs OPPOSITE to what intuition suggests: P(data this extreme | H0 true). It's not the probability of H0, nor of H1, nor a measure of effect size.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Statistical significance isn't practical significance. With a large n, irrelevant differences reach p&lt;0.05; you need to look at effect size and the confidence interval.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — This is the inverse-probability fallacy. P(data|H0) ≠ P(H0|data). Getting P(H0|data) would require a prior and Bayes' theorem (Chapter 136).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Same mistake, in the complementary direction. 1−p is not the probability of the alternative.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-04","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a final inspection, H0 = 'the lot conforms.' In that framing, Type II error corresponds to:</p>","opts":["<p>Using a smaller sample size than the standard requires.</p>","<p>Rejecting a lot that actually conforms — producer's risk.</p>","<p>Accepting a lot that actually doesn't conform — consumer's risk.</p>","<p>Choosing the wrong statistical test for the data type.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Type II error is failing to reject H0 when H0 is false. With H0 = 'lot conforms,' failing to reject means accepting a bad lot — exactly the OC curve's consumer's risk (β).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A small sample increases β, but that isn't the definition of Type II error.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's Type I error (α), the producer's risk. Note the answer depends on how H0 was framed — that's why the scenario states it explicitly.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Choosing the wrong test is a method error, not a Type I/II error, which are probabilistic properties of a correctly specified test.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-05","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Holding everything else constant, a test's statistical power INCREASES when:</p>","opts":["<p>The α level is reduced from 0.05 to 0.01.</p>","<p>The sample size decreases.</p>","<p>The process variability increases.</p>","<p>The minimum effect size to detect (delta) increases.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Power = 1−β. It increases with a larger n, a smaller σ, a larger α, and a larger delta. Detecting a large difference is easier than detecting a small one — so a larger delta implies more power for the same n.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Lowering α makes the rejection criterion stricter, increasing β and reducing power. There's a direct trade-off between α and β for a fixed n.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A smaller sample reduces power: the standard error grows and the distribution under H1 overlaps more with H0's.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — More variability increases the standard error and reduces power. That's why stabilizing the process before testing is good practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-06","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>The Central Limit Theorem justifies which practice in statistical process control?</p>","opts":["<p>Using X̄ charts even when the individual distribution isn't normal.</p>","<p>Skipping the normality test before calculating Cpk.</p>","<p>Treating dependent samples as if they were independent.</p>","<p>Assuming the individual data of any process is normal.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The CLT says the distribution of subgroup MEANS tends toward normal as n grows, even when the individuals aren't normal. That's why the X̄ chart is robust to non-normality — and the I (individuals) chart isn't.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Cpk uses the distribution of the INDIVIDUALS, not of the means. The CLT doesn't protect capability analysis — hence Chapter 72 on non-normal capability.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The CLT requires independence (or weak dependence). It isn't a license to ignore autocorrelation — see Chapter 70.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The CLT says nothing about individual values; it's a statement about the sampling distribution of the mean.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-07","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>When building the OC curve for a sampling plan, when is it mandatory to use the hypergeometric distribution instead of the binomial?</p>","opts":["<p>When the lot is made up of units from several suppliers.</p>","<p>When the acceptance number c is greater than zero.</p>","<p>When the sample is drawn without replacement and n is large relative to the lot size N.</p>","<p>When the AQL is below 1%.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The binomial assumes p is constant across draws (replacement, or an infinite lot). Without replacement, each part drawn changes the remaining proportion. When n/N is appreciable (rule of thumb: n/N &gt; 0.1), the finite-population correction becomes relevant and the hypergeometric is the correct distribution — the so-called type A OC curve.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Lot heterogeneity violates the homogeneous-lot assumption, which is a sampling and lot-definition problem — not fixed by switching distributions.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The value of c doesn't determine the choice of distribution; it only determines the decision criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The AQL sets the reference point on the curve, not the distribution family.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 63</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Acceptance Sampling: Fundamental Concepts and the Operating Characteristic Curve","chapterRef":"cap-063","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-08","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A regression of strength on temperature returns R² = 0.86 and p &lt; 0.001. The engineer concludes that raising the temperature INCREASES the strength. That conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>Unsupported, because an R² below 0.90 is insufficient for any conclusion.</p>","<p>Correct, because a high R² establishes a causal relationship.</p>","<p>Correct, because p &lt; 0.001 rules out chance.</p>","<p>Unsupported, because observational data doesn't separate cause from confounding.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>R² measures how much variance the model explains; p measures evidence against no association. Neither identifies cause. If the high temperature coincides with a different raw-material lot or another shift, the estimated effect is confounded. Only a randomized experiment (or an explicit causal design — Chapter 137) supports the causal claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — There's no R² threshold that authorizes a causal conclusion. The problem is study design, not the magnitude of the fit.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A high R² means a good fit, not causation. A strong confounder produces a high R² and a wrong causal conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The p-value addresses sampling chance, not systematic bias. Confounding isn't reduced by a larger n — on the contrary, it's estimated with more precision.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 46</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Correlation and Regression","chapterRef":"cap-046","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-09","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Before interpreting the p-value of a one-way ANOVA, which set of assumptions must be checked?</p>","opts":["<p>Independence of the residuals, normality of the residuals, and homogeneity of variances.</p>","<p>Normality of the raw data and equal sample sizes.</p>","<p>Linearity and absence of multicollinearity between the factor's levels.</p>","<p>Homoscedasticity only, because ANOVA is robust to everything else.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>ANOVA's three assumptions apply to the RESIDUALS: independence (the most critical and least checked), normality, and homogeneous variances between groups. Independence is guaranteed by the design, not fixable after the fact.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The normality required is of the residuals, not the raw data — groups with different means make the raw data multimodal by construction. Equal sizes help robustness but aren't an assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Multicollinearity is a multiple-regression concept with continuous predictors, not a one-way ANOVA one.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — ANOVA is reasonably robust to non-normality with balanced n, but it is NOT robust to a violation of independence, which is the most serious assumption.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 50</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analysis of Variance (One-Way ANOVA)","chapterRef":"cap-050","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-10","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A flatness characteristic has only an upper specification limit and a strongly right-skewed distribution. Which capability approach is defensible?</p>","opts":["<p>Calculate the normal Cpk and report it with a footnote about the skew.</p>","<p>Fit a suitable distribution (e.g., lognormal or Weibull) and use the percentile method.</p>","<p>Transform the data until the normality test passes, and report the transformed index as if it were the original.</p>","<p>Discard extreme values until the distribution becomes symmetric.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The percentile approach (ISO/Clements method) replaces μ±3σ with the 0.135% and 99.865% percentiles of the fitted distribution, preserving the interpretation as a nonconforming fraction. Flatness is geometrically bounded at zero and skewed by nature — a lognormal or Weibull fit is physically plausible, not a numerical trick.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A normal Cpk on skewed data poorly estimates the tail, which is exactly what matters: the index can look acceptable while the real out-of-spec fraction is several times larger.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Transformation is legitimate (Box-Cox, Johnson), but the index has to be reported identifying the transformation and reconverting the limits — presenting it as the original misleads the customer.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Removing extremes with no identified assignable cause is data manipulation. In a PPAP audit, that's a serious nonconformity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 72</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability for Non-Normal and Attribute Data","chapterRef":"cap-072","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-11","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In an X̄-R chart with subgroups of n = 5, the mean range is R̄ = 4.64 and d₂ = 2.326. What is the estimate of the within-subgroup standard deviation?</p>","opts":["<p>0.50</p>","<p>1.99</p>","<p>2.33</p>","<p>10.79</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>σ̂_within = R̄/d₂ = 4.64/2.326 ≈ 1.99. This is the short-term variation estimate used in the control limits and in calculating Cp/Cpk.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.50 doesn't come from any valid operation on the given values.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 2.33 is approximately d₂ itself — confusing the constant with the result is a common mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 10.79 = R̄ × d₂; the operation is inverted (multiplication instead of division).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-12","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a Weibull fit to times to failure, the estimated shape parameter is β ≈ 0.6. The reliability reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The fit is invalid, because β must be greater than 1.</p>","<p>Wear-out failures; scheduled-replacement preventive maintenance is indicated.</p>","<p>Decreasing failure rate — infant mortality; burn-in and supplier process control are indicated.</p>","<p>Constant failure rate; preventive replacement brings no benefit.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In the Weibull, β&lt;1 means a DECREASING failure rate over time — the initial branch of the bathtub curve, typical of manufacturing defects. Preventively replacing a part in that regime is counterproductive: the new part has a HIGHER failure risk than the one that has been in service for a while.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — β&lt;1 is a perfectly valid, informative result; nothing in the Weibull requires β&gt;1.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Wear-out corresponds to β&gt;1 (increasing rate). With β=0.6, scheduled replacement makes reliability worse.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A constant rate is exactly β=1 (the memoryless exponential case).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 47</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Non-Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-047","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-53","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>'Risk-based thinking,' in ISO 9001, requires the organization to:</p>","opts":["<p>Determine risks and opportunities that affect conformity and satisfaction, plan proportional actions, and evaluate their effectiveness.</p>","<p>Calculate the RPN for every process.</p>","<p>Hire a certified risk manager.</p>","<p>Implement ISO 31000 and maintain a formal risk register.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The standard requires the logic (determine → plan a proportional action → integrate into the process → evaluate effectiveness) and leaves the METHOD to the organization. Choosing and justifying a method proportional to the risk is an engineering competency; turning a specific tool into a universal requirement is a misreading of the standard.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — RPN is an FMEA metric — and even within FMEA, the AIAG-VDA reference replaced it with Action Priority.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — No standard in the series requires a specific job title or credential.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — ISO 9001 doesn't require ISO 31000 or a formal risk register, though both can be good choices.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 80</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Fundamentals and Types of Risk Management","chapterRef":"cap-080","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-54","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>The AIAG-VDA reference's Action Priority (AP) replaced the RPN mainly because the RPN:</p>","opts":["<p>Was too slow to calculate.</p>","<p>Collapses very different combinations into the same number and treats severity, occurrence, and detection as interchangeable.</p>","<p>Could only be used in PFMEA.</p>","<p>Wasn't accepted by ISO standards.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>S=10, O=1, D=1 gives RPN=10, same as S=1, O=10, D=1. These are radically different situations: the first is a potentially catastrophic failure and the second a frequent nuisance. AP uses a logic table that prioritizes severity first and then occurrence, preventing that swap.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Calculation time was never the issue; multiplication is trivial.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — RPN was used in both DFMEA and PFMEA indistinctly.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The issue is technical and methodological, not one of normative acceptance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 81</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA — What It Is and How to Build It","chapterRef":"cap-081","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-55","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>RESIDUAL risk is distinguished from INHERENT risk because residual is:</p>","opts":["<p>The risk transferred to the insurer.</p>","<p>The risk before any control is considered.</p>","<p>The risk that remains after accounting for the effectiveness of existing or planned controls.</p>","<p>Always equal to zero in a certified system.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Inherent = risk with no controls (or ignoring them). Residual = what's left after the controls act, given their real effectiveness. The gap between the two is the measure of the control system's value — and comparing them is what lets you decide whether the treatment is worth the cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Transfer is one of the treatment strategies; the risk that remains after transfer (including counterparty risk) is still residual.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's the definition of inherent risk.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Certification doesn't eliminate risk; zero residual risk is impossible, and claiming it is a sign of a superficial analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 143</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Engineering Architecture — Context, Taxonomy, Appetite, and Criteria","chapterRef":"cap-143","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-56","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>To analyze how multiple combined failures can lead to a single undesired safety event, and quantify its probability, the most suitable technique is:</p>","opts":["<p>Pareto chart</p>","<p>Control chart</p>","<p>Ishikawa diagram</p>","<p>Fault Tree Analysis (FTA)</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>FTA is deductive (it starts from the top event and works down to the causes) and represents combinatorial logic through AND/OR gates, allowing quantitative calculation of the top event's probability from the basic events' probabilities. It's the indicated technique for safety events with multiple combined causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Pareto prioritizes causes that already occurred and were counted; it doesn't model potential combinations.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A control chart monitors stability over time; it doesn't analyze an event's causal structure.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ishikawa organizes and prompts the generation of possible causes, but doesn't represent combinatorial logic or support calculation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 144</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Risk Identification and Analysis Techniques (IEC 31010)","chapterRef":"cap-144","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CQE-57","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A risk matrix (probability × impact) classifies a risk as 'low probability, catastrophic impact.' Compared to a risk classified as 'high probability, low impact' that occupies the same aggregate score on the matrix, the correct treatment is:</p>","opts":["<p>Different in KIND even with a similar aggregate score: high-severity/low-probability risks typically require a contingency plan and monitoring, while high-probability/low-severity risks are managed through routine process control.</p>","<p>Identical, because both occupy the same score on the matrix.</p>","<p>Always lower for the catastrophic risk, because low probability dominates the score.</p>","<p>Irrelevant, because the matrix only compares risks of similar probability.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The matrix's aggregate score is a useful summary, but it hides the risk's nature. A rare, catastrophic risk calls for contingency, reserved resources, and early-warning monitoring; a frequent, low-impact risk calls for routine statistical control. Treating both the same way because they have \"the same score\" is the most expensive mistake in reading a risk matrix mechanically.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The same aggregate score doesn't imply the same treatment strategy; the risk's nature determines that, not just the number.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Automatically lowering the priority because probability is low is exactly the mistake the matrix exists to prevent when severity is catastrophic.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Comparing risks with different combinations of probability and severity is precisely what the matrix is for.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CQE-58","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>The Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC) is used mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Prioritize processes by the cost of nonconformance.</p>","<p>Anticipate, for an ongoing plan, what can go wrong at each step and define countermeasures before implementation.</p>","<p>Replace FMEA in low-risk processes.</p>","<p>Calculate the process capability index before startup.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The PDPC is one of the seven management and planning tools. It maps out the steps of a plan and, for each one, asks \"what could go wrong here?\", defining the countermeasure before the plan moves forward — structured contingency planning done before implementation, not after the incident.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Prioritizing by cost is a prioritization-matrix function, not the PDPC's.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The PDPC doesn't replace FMEA: one analyzes product/process failure modes, the other anticipates deviations in a plan or project. They're complementary.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Capability is calculated from data on a process already running, not from a planning chart.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 86</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Tree Diagram and Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC)","chapterRef":"cap-086","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-59","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>Under the classic risk-treatment logic (ISO 31000), transferring a risk to an insurer or subcontractor:</p>","opts":["<p>Eliminates the inherent risk entirely.</p>","<p>Is equivalent to accepting it, because the organization no longer has any exposure.</p>","<p>Reduces the organization's direct financial exposure, but doesn't eliminate the operational risk or the counterparty risk — the residual risk remains and must still be monitored.</p>","<p>Is only a valid option for low-severity risks.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Transferring reallocates the FINANCIAL CONSEQUENCE, not the hazard itself: the accident, the shutdown, or the reputational damage still happens operationally. And a new risk appears — counterparty risk (the insurer not paying, the subcontractor failing). The residual risk from the transfer still has to stay on the risk register.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — No treatment strategy eliminates the inherent risk entirely; it reduces, transfers, or accepts its consequence.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Transferring and accepting are distinct strategies: accepting is not acting on the risk; transferring is contractually reallocating the financial consequence.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Transfer (insurance, contractual guarantees) is frequently used precisely for high-severity risks.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CQE-60","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>FMECA (Failure Mode, Effects and Criticality Analysis) is distinguished from conventional FMEA because FMECA:</p>","opts":["<p>Replaces the Severity × Occurrence × Detection scale with a binary pass/fail scale.</p>","<p>Only applies to processes, never to products.</p>","<p>Eliminates the need to identify the failure modes' causes.</p>","<p>Adds a formal, quantified criticality analysis, typically combining severity with the failure mode's probability of occurrence into a dedicated criticality metric.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>FMECA (originating in the military standard MIL-STD-1629A) builds on FMEA and adds a formal criticality analysis — often a dedicated severity × probability matrix — to quantify how critical each failure mode is, beyond the RPN or Action Priority already familiar from FMEA.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — FMECA adds granularity and quantification, it doesn't remove it; it doesn't simplify to binary.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — FMECA applies to both products and processes, with the same flexibility as FMEA.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Identifying causes remains central to the analysis; criticality is an added dimension, not a replacement.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 83</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMECA — Failure Mode, Effects, and Criticality Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-083","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-01","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>When selecting Six Sigma projects for a portfolio, which combination of criteria is most defensible?</p>","opts":["<p>The project that uses the largest number of BoK tools.</p>","<p>Linked to a strategic objective, a measurable problem with a baseline, an unknown cause, and a scope executable in 3-6 months.</p>","<p>The project the sponsor personally values the most.</p>","<p>The project with the highest estimated return, regardless of whether data exists.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>A good DMAIC project needs, simultaneously, relevance (ties to strategy), measurability (a baseline exists), genuine uncertainty (if the cause is already known, it's implementation, not DMAIC), and time feasibility. Failing any one of these is the origin of most projects that die halfway through.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Number of tools is an indicator of methodological excess, not of value. If a Pareto and a poka-yoke solve it, the project is done.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Sponsorship is necessary but not sufficient; without a measurable problem there's no way to demonstrate improvement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — An estimated return with no data is an opinion with decimal places. Without a baseline there's no way to validate the gain at the end.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 32</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Selecting the Right Projects","chapterRef":"cap-032","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-02","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>The most substantive criticism of the traditional 'belt' system is that:</p>","opts":["<p>The exams are too expensive.</p>","<p>The belts require too many hours of training.</p>","<p>It certifies generic theoretical knowledge instead of demonstrated competence on real problems.</p>","<p>The belts aren't recognized internationally.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The criticism is about what the credential measures. An exam-based certification verifies that the person recognizes tools; it doesn't verify that they can frame a real problem, choose a method, defend assumptions, and sustain the gain. Hence the proposal for quality katas: certifying concrete, observable skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cost is a practical barrier, not the methodological criticism.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Training hours vary widely between providers and aren't the core of the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Certifications like the ASQ CSSBB have broad international acceptance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 42</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"After the Project: Quality Katas, Coaching, and the Digitalization of Continuous Improvement","chapterRef":"cap-042","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-03","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>A Six Sigma project's ROI calculation should be validated by Finance mainly because:</p>","opts":["<p>The Black Belt isn't competent enough to do the math.</p>","<p>It's required by the ISO 9001 standard.</p>","<p>Finance is responsible for approving the project's closure.</p>","<p>Real cash gains (hard savings) need to be distinguished from avoided cost or freed-up capacity (soft savings), under consistent accounting rules.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Without shared rules, every project counts however it wants, and the summed 'gains' exceed the company's actual result — the best-known credibility problem in Six Sigma programs. Independent validation by Finance defines what counts as a gain and prevents double counting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The issue isn't arithmetic competence, it's independence and consistency of criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — ISO 9001 doesn't require financial validation of improvement projects.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Closure normally belongs to the sponsor and the process owner; Finance validates the value, not the technical conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 32</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Selecting the Right Projects","chapterRef":"cap-032","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CSSBB-04","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>In a Lean Six Sigma transformation, the obstacle that most often makes it fail is:</p>","opts":["<p>A shortage of statistical software.</p>","<p>The absence of sustained leadership involvement and of protected time for the teams.</p>","<p>Lack of a reference manual.</p>","<p>Too much available data.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Programs die when resources are symbolic: a part-time Black Belt, with no protected time, reporting to someone who isn't accountable for the result, and leadership that sponsors at kickoff and disappears during execution. Tools and training are the easy part.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Free, capable alternatives exist (Python, R); tooling is rarely the constraint.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Reference material is abundant and doesn't determine the success of organizational change.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — An abundance of data is an opportunity; the problem is data quality and governance, not quantity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 28</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean Culture, Leadership, and Organizational Change Management","chapterRef":"cap-028","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-05","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A process has 4 defect opportunities per unit. Across 500 units, 20 defects were observed. What is the DPMO?</p>","opts":["<p>10,000</p>","<p>40,000</p>","<p>5,000</p>","<p>2,000</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>DPMO = defects / (units × opportunities) × 1,000,000 = 20/(500×4) × 10⁶ = 20/2000 × 10⁶ = 10,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 40,000 forgets to multiply by the opportunities in the denominator (it would use 500 alone... and still gets the scale wrong).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 5,000 would correspond to 8 opportunities per unit.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 2,000 is the denominator (500×4), not the DPMO.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-06","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>Rolled Throughput Yield (RTY) differs from final yield because it:</p>","opts":["<p>Is always higher than the final yield.</p>","<p>Only considers the last step of the process.</p>","<p>Multiplies the first-pass yields of every step, exposing the 'hidden factory' of rework.</p>","<p>Is calculated on cost instead of on units.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Final yield counts what ships good, including what was reworked. RTY = ∏FTYᵢ counts only what passed FIRST TIME at each step. The gap between the two is the invisible cost of rework — the hidden factory.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's always LOWER than or equal to final yield, precisely because it penalizes intermediate rework.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It considers every step; that's the whole point of the indicator.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It's calculated on units/opportunities; translating it into cost is a later step.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-07","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>In a y = f(x) relationship applied to process improvement, the most common strategic mistake is:</p>","opts":["<p>Using too many independent variables in the model.</p>","<p>Logging data at too high a frequency.</p>","<p>Choosing y as a continuous variable.</p>","<p>Measuring and controlling y (the outcome) instead of identifying and controlling the vital x's.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Controlling y is inspection: you find the problem after it's already been created. All of Six Sigma rests on drilling down from y to the x's, proving which ones are vital, and shifting control to them — which is where prevention is possible and cheap.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Too many predictors is a modeling problem (multicollinearity, overfitting), not the central strategic mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — High frequency can introduce autocorrelation, but that's a technical and fixable problem.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A continuous y is usually preferable: it gives more information per observation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 5</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"What Is a Process?","chapterRef":"cap-005","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-08","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A team wants to replace control charts with a Power BI dashboard updated hourly. The correct technical assessment is:</p>","opts":["<p>The dashboard shows status; it doesn't implement the statistical detection rules or distinguish common cause from special cause. They're complementary tools, not substitutes.</p>","<p>It's only acceptable if the dashboard updates in real time.</p>","<p>It doesn't matter, as long as the data is the same.</p>","<p>Good replacement: the dashboard is more visible and more current.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>A line chart with colored targets leads people to react to noise (tampering), which increases variation — Deming's funnel experiment. The control chart exists precisely to answer the question 'is this signal or noise?' The dashboard distributes information; the chart decides whether action is needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Update frequency doesn't fix the absence of detection rules.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The same data with the wrong decision rule produces wrong decisions faster.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Visibility and currency don't replace the statistical decision criterion.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 106</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Data Architecture and the Tool-Choice Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-106","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-09","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>On a project team, a senior member dominates meetings and silences the rest. The most appropriate facilitation intervention is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Remove the member from the team.</p>","<p>Use structured participation techniques (nominal group technique, brainwriting, round-robin) that make everyone's contribution explicit.</p>","<p>Ignore it, because their experience compensates for it.</p>","<p>Report the behavior to their manager.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Structured methods solve the problem through the meeting's design, with no personal confrontation: with brainwriting or NGT, ideas come in parallel and in writing, and verbal dominance stops having any effect. It's the facilitation intervention with the lowest relational cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Removing them loses valuable technical knowledge and sends a bad signal to the team; it's a last resort.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ignoring it produces apparent consensus — the worst outcome, because the team stops surfacing dissenting information.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Escalating a meeting-dynamics problem up the chain is disproportionate and damages trust.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-10","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>In a RACI matrix, the difference between 'Accountable' and 'Responsible' is that:</p>","opts":["<p>Accountable is always the financial sponsor.</p>","<p>They're the same role under different names.</p>","<p>Accountable is answerable for the outcome and is a single person; Responsible does the work and can be more than one person.</p>","<p>Accountable does the work and Responsible approves it.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The practical rule is that there can only be ONE 'A' per deliverable — if there are two, there's effectively none. The 'R' can be shared across several doers. Confusing the two is the most common cause of orphaned deliverables.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It can be the sponsor, but it's often the process owner; the role is defined by accountability, not by the budget.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They're distinct roles, and that distinction is the whole point of the tool.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It's reversed: Responsible does the work, Accountable answers for it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-11","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>In a brainstorming session, the rule that contributes most to the quantity and diversity of ideas is:</p>","opts":["<p>Requiring every idea to come with a cost estimate.</p>","<p>Recording only the ideas that get immediate consensus.</p>","<p>Limiting the group to subject-matter experts.</p>","<p>Separating generation from evaluation: no idea is criticized during the generation phase.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Evaluating during generation activates self-censorship: people filter before speaking, and the unconventional ideas — the ones that carry the value — never get said. Separating the two phases in time is the technique's founding rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Requiring a cost estimate is evaluation in disguise, and it stalls generation exactly like criticism does.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Immediate consensus eliminates the divergent ideas, which are the session's raw material.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Experts only reduces cognitive diversity; an outside observer sees what the expert has stopped seeing out of habit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 11</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Facilitation","chapterRef":"cap-011","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-12","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>When communicating statistical results to leadership, the most effective practice is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Start with the recommended business decision and its consequence, keeping the statistical detail available as backup.</p>","<p>Avoid any numbers, so as not to confuse the audience.</p>","<p>Use only p-values, since they're the most objective evidence.</p>","<p>Present every software output, to demonstrate rigor.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Leadership decides on risk, cost, and time. The effective structure is: recommendation → supporting evidence → remaining uncertainty → what changes if the decision is delayed. Technical detail should be available and defensible, but it isn't the opening.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Omitting numbers prevents an informed decision; the problem isn't having numbers, it's how they're framed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Isolated p-values don't communicate magnitude, uncertainty, or economic consequence — exactly what the decision requires.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Dumping software output shifts the interpretation work onto people who can't do it, and undermines credibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 12</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Communication Skills","chapterRef":"cap-012","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-13","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>The role of a coach in a continuous-improvement program is distinguished from a trainer's because the coach:</p>","opts":["<p>Only checks whether the tools were applied correctly.</p>","<p>Works with the person on a real, ongoing problem, developing their problem-solving reasoning, not just their tool knowledge.</p>","<p>Is always external to the organization.</p>","<p>Takes responsibility for the project's outcome.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Training transfers knowledge; coaching develops the ability to apply it under uncertainty, on the actual problem, with iterative feedback. That's what distinguishes an improvement kata from a course — and what makes the knowledge survive after training ends.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Checking for correct application is a method audit, a small fraction of coaching.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — An internal coach is often the most effective, because they know the context.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Responsibility for the outcome remains with the project leader and the process owner; the coach answers for the person's development.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 42</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"After the Project: Quality Katas, Coaching, and the Digitalization of Continuous Improvement","chapterRef":"cap-042","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-14","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A solid project charter must, without exception, contain:</p>","opts":["<p>The report from the last internal audit.</p>","<p>The proposed solution and the implementation schedule.</p>","<p>A problem statement with a baseline, a scope with explicit boundaries, a measurable goal, a business case, a team, and a sponsor.</p>","<p>The complete list of statistical tools to be used.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The charter is the project's contract. It has to say what the problem is (with a number), how far the project goes — and where it does NOT go (the defense against scope creep) — what it aims to achieve, why it's worth doing, and who's accountable for what.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — An audit report can be a source of the problem, but it isn't a charter element.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Including the solution in the charter is the structural mistake: the remedy gets defined before the diagnosis, and DMAIC becomes theater.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Tools emerge from the nature of the data and the question; fixing them at the start puts method ahead of the problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-15","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>SIPOC is used mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Calculate the process capability.</p>","<p>Prioritize root causes.</p>","<p>Detail every micro-step of the process with cycle times.</p>","<p>Establish the high-level scope — suppliers, inputs, process, outputs, and customers — and align the team on where the process starts and ends.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>SIPOC's value is in the conversation it forces at the start of the project: where does the process begin and end. It's common to discover there that two team members had different boundaries in mind — and would have worked on different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Capability requires measurement data and statistical analysis, not a scoping diagram.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Cause prioritization happens in the Analyze phase, with data.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That's the detailed process map or the value stream map, later, finer-grained tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-16","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A strong problem statement should avoid:</p>","opts":["<p>The presumed cause and an implied solution.</p>","<p>Identifying the affected process.</p>","<p>Specific numbers and dates.</p>","<p>Any reference to the impact on the customer.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Including a cause or a solution in the statement closes off the investigation space before there's any data. 'Line 3's scrap rate rose from 2.1% to 5.4% between January and June, at a cost of €180k' is strong. 'Line 3 needs a new machine' is a solution disguised as a problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Identifying the process is essential to bound the scope.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Numbers and dates are exactly what makes the statement strong and verifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Customer and business impact justifies the project's existence and should be included.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 91</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 9001 in 2026","chapterRef":"cap-091","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-17","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A project that started focused on a warehouse's delivery times now also includes renegotiating with carriers and changing the ERP. This is a symptom of:</p>","opts":["<p>A natural consequence of DMAIC.</p>","<p>Scope creep — uncontrolled scope expansion, which should be handled with an In/Out-of-Scope matrix and a formal charter revision.</p>","<p>Good systems-thinking practice.</p>","<p>A sign that the original problem was poorly measured.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Each expansion looks reasonable in isolation; added together, they make the project unexecutable within the deadline. The response isn't to refuse on principle, it's to decide formally: it goes into the charter with a revised deadline and resources, or it becomes a separate project. The In/Out-of-Scope matrix documents that boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — DMAIC requires a stable scope; scope creep is a pathology, not a step.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Systems thinking is about understanding interdependencies; it doesn't require solving everything in a single project.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That can happen, but nothing in the scenario suggests a measurement problem — it suggests an undefended boundary.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 92</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 19011:2026 — Management System Auditing","chapterRef":"cap-092","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-18","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A CTQ tree serves to:</p>","opts":["<p>Represent the organization's hierarchical structure.</p>","<p>List a product's failure modes.</p>","<p>Decompose a broad customer need into requirements (drivers) and those, in turn, into measurable characteristics with a specification.</p>","<p>Rank causes by frequency of occurrence.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>'Fast delivery' isn't measurable or controllable. The CTQ tree drills down from need → driver → measure with a target and limits ('shipping time ≤ 4 h on 95% of orders'). Without that drill-down, VOC never reaches the process.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The CTQ tree is about customer requirements, not an org chart.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Failure modes are the FMEA.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Ranking by frequency is the Pareto.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 52</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Logistic Regression: Modeling Conforming / Nonconforming","chapterRef":"cap-052","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-19","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>In a stakeholder analysis, a stakeholder with HIGH power and LOW interest should be:</p>","opts":["<p>Involved in every operational meeting.</p>","<p>Treated as the project's sponsor.</p>","<p>Ignored, since their interest is low.</p>","<p>Kept satisfied and informed at the appropriate level, because they can block the project if caught off guard.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The power/interest grid gives four strategies: manage closely (high/high), keep satisfied (high/low), keep informed (low/high), monitor (low/low). Low interest doesn't mean irrelevant: it means they only react when something affects them — and at that point, the power they hold becomes decisive.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Involving them in everything wastes their time and drains the attention you want available for critical moments.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The sponsor needs high power AND high interest; low interest is incompatible with effective sponsorship.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ignoring someone with blocking power is the mistake that kills technically sound projects at approval.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-20","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>An operational definition of 'scratched part' should specify:</p>","opts":["<p>The observation method, the conditions (lighting, distance, magnification), the acceptance criterion, and who's qualified to apply it.</p>","<p>The name of the responsible inspector.</p>","<p>The cost of the defect.</p>","<p>Only the applicable dimensional tolerance.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Without an operational definition, two inspectors classify the same part differently and no downstream analysis is trustworthy. It has to be specific enough that two independent people reach the same classification — a criterion later tested with attribute MSA.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Identifying the person doesn't define the criterion; the definition has to be independent of who's measuring.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Cost matters for prioritization, not for classification.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A dimensional tolerance doesn't define a visual attribute like a surface scratch.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 35</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measure","chapterRef":"cap-035","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-21","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A Gage R&amp;R ANOVA attributes 8% of total variation to repeatability and 26% to reproducibility. The priority action is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Replace the measurement instrument with a higher-resolution one.</p>","<p>Standardize the method and train the operators, because the larger source is the variation BETWEEN appraisers.</p>","<p>Increase the number of parts in the study.</p>","<p>Accept the system, since each component is below 30%.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Repeatability is the equipment (same operator, same part); reproducibility is the operator (between appraisers). A dominant reproducibility points to an ambiguous method, inconsistent fixturing, or training — organizational fixes, typically far cheaper than buying a new instrument.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Swapping the instrument targets repeatability, which is the smaller component here; it spends capital without addressing the dominant cause.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — More parts improve the estimate of part-to-part variation, they don't reduce the measurement system's variation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The criterion applies to the combined %GRR, not to each component in isolation — and the combined value here clearly exceeds what's acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CSSBB-22","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>To estimate a proportion with a margin of error of ±2% at 95% confidence, with no prior knowledge of the proportion, the conservative assumption is to use:</p>","opts":["<p>p = 0, because there's no information.</p>","<p>p = 0.01, as the most favorable scenario.</p>","<p>p = 0.50, because it maximizes p(1−p) and therefore the required sample size.</p>","<p>p = 0.95, matching the confidence level.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The variance of a proportion is p(1−p)/n, maximized at p = 0.50. Using 0.50 guarantees the sample is sufficient regardless of the true proportion — it's the standard conservative choice absent prior information.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — p = 0 zeroes out the variance and leads to n = 0, which is absurd.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — p = 0.01 minimizes the variance and produces a sample too small if the true proportion is larger.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Confusing the confidence level with the estimated proportion is a conceptual mistake: they're parameters of different natures.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-23","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A histogram of service times shows two distinct peaks. The most likely interpretation is:</p>","opts":["<p>A normal distribution with high kurtosis.</p>","<p>A sample too small to interpret.</p>","<p>A measurement error in the collection system.</p>","<p>A mix of two distinct populations (e.g., two order types or two shifts) that should be stratified before any further analysis.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Bimodality is almost always the signature of missing stratification. Calculating a mean and standard deviation over the mix produces a mean that describes neither group. Separating the populations is often, by itself, half of the project's discovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Kurtosis describes the weight of a unimodal distribution's tails; it doesn't produce two peaks.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Two clear, separated modes aren't an artifact of a small sample; and the fix would be collecting more data, not discarding the pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Measurement error typically produces spread, impossible values, or step-wise rounding, not two clean modes.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-24","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A normal process has μ = 100 and σ = 5. Approximately what proportion of units is above 110?</p>","opts":["<p>0.0228</p>","<p>0.1587</p>","<p>0.3173</p>","<p>0.0013</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>z = (110−100)/5 = 2.0. P(Z &gt; 2) ≈ 0.0228 (2.28%).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 0.1587 corresponds to z = 1.0 (one standard deviation).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 0.3173 is the TWO-sided probability for z = ±1.0.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 0.0013 corresponds to z = 3.0.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-25","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>The difference between Cp/Cpk and Pp/Ppk lies in:</p>","opts":["<p>Cp/Cpk use σ estimated from the variation WITHIN subgroups (short-term); Pp/Ppk use the overall sample standard deviation (long-term).</p>","<p>Cp/Cpk apply to attribute data and Pp/Ppk to variables.</p>","<p>Cp/Cpk require normality and Pp/Ppk don't.</p>","<p>They're identical; the difference is just naming, between AIAG and ISO.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The denominator is the whole difference. σ_within (via R̄/d₂ or s̄/c₄) captures short-term variation; σ_overall also includes the drift between subgroups. Ppk ≪ Cpk is the diagnostic of a process with between-subgroup variation — drift, adjustments, lot changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both pairs apply to variables data.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Both assume normality (or require an alternative treatment, Chapter 72).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The distinction is substantive, and it's exactly what PPAP examines.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-26","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>Before analyzing a quality-data extract, the MOST important check is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Confirm the file opens correctly in the software.</p>","<p>Profile completeness, uniqueness, validity, consistency, accuracy, and timeliness — and verify the field definition matches the process's.</p>","<p>Sort the data chronologically.</p>","<p>Convert every column to text.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Most wrong conclusions in quality analytics are born before any statistics happen: a field that means something different than assumed, non-random missing records, reprocessing duplicates, mixed units. Profiling the data is applying quality to the data itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Opening the file is a trivial prerequisite, not a quality check.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Sorting is a display convenience; it validates nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Converting to text destroys types and blocks numeric validation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 109</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Data Quality: Applying Quality to the Data Itself","chapterRef":"cap-109","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-27","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>Missing data classified as MNAR (missing not at random) is characterized by:</p>","opts":["<p>Missing data that can always be mean-imputed with no bias.</p>","<p>The probability of missingness doesn't depend on anything observable or on the value itself.</p>","<p>The probability of missingness depends on the UNOBSERVED value itself, even after conditioning on the observed variables.</p>","<p>The probability of missingness depends only on observed variables.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>MNAR is the most serious case, and the most common one in industrial practice: the test that wasn't logged because the result was bad. No standard imputation method fixes it, because the needed information isn't in the data — the solution requires changing the collection process or explicitly modeling the missingness mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Mean imputation biases the estimate and always underestimates the variance, even under MCAR.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's the definition of MCAR, the benign case.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That's the definition of MAR, treatable with multiple imputation or weighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 134</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Data Scientist Statistical Reasoning in Quality","chapterRef":"cap-134","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-28","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A scrap-prediction model reaches an AUC of 0.97 in validation. Checking the variables, one of them turns out to be the disposition code logged AFTER inspection. This is a case of:</p>","opts":["<p>Multicollinearity between predictors.</p>","<p>Class imbalance.</p>","<p>Overfitting from too many parameters.</p>","<p>Data leakage: the variable isn't available at prediction time, and the performance won't reproduce in production.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Leakage is the most common cause of models that shine in validation and fail in production. The decisive test is temporal: at the moment the prediction is actually needed, does this variable already exist? If not, the model is 'predicting' the past.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Multicollinearity affects coefficient interpretation, it doesn't artificially inflate performance.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Class imbalance is a different problem, and it typically lowers useful metrics rather than inflating them.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Overfitting degrades validation performance; here the validation is excellent — the problem is that the validation itself is contaminated.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 111</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Machine Learning Applied to Quality: Where It Helps and Where It Misleads","chapterRef":"cap-111","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-29","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A study's EXPERIMENTAL unit is defined as:</p>","opts":["<p>The smallest entity to which the treatment is applied independently.</p>","<p>Always the individual part measured.</p>","<p>The largest lot available for analysis.</p>","<p>The production shift.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Confusing the experimental unit with the MEASUREMENT unit is the source of pseudoreplication: if the treatment is applied to the whole oven and 50 parts from that oven are measured, the effective n is 1 oven, not 50 parts. Treating 50 as n drastically underestimates the standard error and produces false significance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The part is often the measurement unit, not the experimental one — that distinction is exactly the point.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Lot size doesn't define the independence of the treatment application.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It can be, in some designs, but it isn't the general definition.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 62</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Integrity: Random Sampling, Selection Bias, and Chain of Custody","chapterRef":"cap-062","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-30","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>To propagate the uncertainty of 5 component dimensions into an assembly clearance, with different distributions and a nonlinear physical limit, the most suitable approach is:</p>","opts":["<p>Worst-case stacking.</p>","<p>Monte Carlo simulation, sampling each component's distribution and propagating through the assembly function.</p>","<p>A two-sample t-test.</p>","<p>An X̄-R control chart of the clearance.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Monte Carlo naturally handles heterogeneous distributions and nonlinearity, and returns the FULL distribution of the clearance — giving access to percentiles and the out-of-tolerance fraction, not just a nominal value. It's what neither worst-case nor RSS can do when nonlinearity is present.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Worst-case ignores the distributions and is extremely conservative; with 5 components the result is practically useless.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A t-test compares two means; it doesn't propagate uncertainty through a function.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The chart monitors the clearance after assembly; it doesn't predict it from the components at the design stage.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 140</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Uncertainty Quantification and Monte Carlo for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-140","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-31","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>A one-way ANOVA with 4 levels returns p = 0.002. The correct conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>The factor explains 99.8% of the variation.</p>","<p>All four levels differ from each other.</p>","<p>At least one pair of means differs; a post-hoc test is needed to identify which.</p>","<p>The level with the highest mean is the best one.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>ANOVA is an OMNIBUS test: it rejects the hypothesis that all means are equal, without saying which ones differ. Identifying the pairs requires a post-hoc procedure (Tukey, for example) that controls the family-wise error rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 1−p isn't the proportion of variance explained — that's R² or eta-squared.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It's possible that only one level differs from the other three, which are equal to each other.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The highest sample mean may not differ significantly from the second; and 'best' depends on the engineering objective, not the statistics.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 50</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analysis of Variance (One-Way ANOVA)","chapterRef":"cap-050","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CSSBB-32","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a multiple regression, one predictor has VIF = 12. The correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The predictor should always be removed from the model.</p>","<p>The model violates the normality assumption.</p>","<p>The predictor is the most important one in the model.</p>","<p>Severe multicollinearity: the coefficient is unstable and its standard error is inflated, but the model's overall predictive ability may still hold up.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>VIF &gt; 10 indicates the coefficient's variance is inflated roughly 12-fold relative to no collinearity. The coefficient's sign can even flip with small changes in the data. For PREDICTION that can be tolerable; for causal INTERPRETATION of coefficients, it isn't.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Blindly removing it can eliminate relevant information. Alternatives: centering, combining variables, penalized regression, or collecting data that breaks the collinearity.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — VIF is about the relationship between predictors, not about the residuals' distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — VIF says nothing about importance; it measures redundancy with the other predictors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 51</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Multiple Regression: Diagnostics, Multicollinearity, and Model Selection","chapterRef":"cap-051","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CSSBB-33","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>The response of interest is conforming/nonconforming and there are three continuous process predictors. The appropriate model is:</p>","opts":["<p>Logistic regression, which models the log-odds of the probability of nonconformance.</p>","<p>One-way ANOVA.</p>","<p>A u chart.</p>","<p>Simple linear regression on the observed proportion.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Logistic regression respects the binary nature of the response: it keeps the predicted probabilities within [0,1] and assumes the correct binomial variance. Linear regression on a binary response can predict negative probabilities or ones above 1, and violates homoscedasticity.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — ANOVA requires a continuous response and a categorical factor; here the response is binary and the predictors are continuous.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A u chart is a monitoring tool for counts, not a modeling tool for relationships between variables.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Modeling proportions with linear regression produces predictions outside the valid range and incorrect standard errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 52</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Logistic Regression: Modeling Conforming / Nonconforming","chapterRef":"cap-052","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-34","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>When testing 20 process characteristics simultaneously with α = 0.05 each, the probability of at least one false positive is approximately:</p>","opts":["<p>5%</p>","<p>64%</p>","<p>20%</p>","<p>1%</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>1 − (0.95)²⁰ ≈ 1 − 0.358 = 0.642, or about 64%. That's why multiple testing requires controlling the family-wise error rate (Bonferroni) or the false discovery rate (Benjamini-Hochberg, more powerful when there are many tests).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 5% is the PER-TEST rate; the family-wise rate grows quickly with the number of tests.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 20% would be 20×0.05/5, a calculation with no basis; the accumulation is multiplicative, not linear.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 1% would be a reduced α, not the accumulated probability.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 135</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Bootstrap, Permutation, Power, Effect Size and Multiple Testing","chapterRef":"cap-135","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-35","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>The bootstrap is particularly useful when:</p>","opts":["<p>There is MNAR missing data.</p>","<p>You want to artificially increase the actual sample size.</p>","<p>The sampling distribution of the statistic of interest is unknown or has no closed form (e.g., the median, a percentile, a ratio).</p>","<p>The data is perfectly normal and n is large.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>By resampling with replacement from the observed sample, the bootstrap empirically approximates the sampling distribution without needing an analytical formula. It's the right tool for confidence intervals on statistics like the median, P95, or Cpk, where the classic formula doesn't exist or is fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The bootstrap doesn't fix selection bias or MNAR — the problem is in the data-generating mechanism, not in the statistic.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The bootstrap doesn't create new information. It resamples existing information; with a small, non-representative n, it inherits the same problem.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — With normality and a large n, the classical formulas are already adequate, and simpler.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 135</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Bootstrap, Permutation, Power, Effect Size and Multiple Testing","chapterRef":"cap-135","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-36","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In an A/B test of two machine configurations, randomization serves mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Reduce the cost of the experiment.</p>","<p>Guarantee normality of the residuals.</p>","<p>Increase the observed effect size.</p>","<p>Balance, on average, both known AND unknown confounders across groups, enabling a causal interpretation.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Randomization's unique strength is that it acts on variables the experimenter doesn't even know exist. No downstream statistical adjustment can do that: you can only adjust for what you measured. That's what separates causal evidence from associative evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It often increases operational cost (more setup changeovers); it's justified by validity, not economy.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The normality of the residuals depends on the data-generating mechanism, not on random assignment.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Randomization doesn't change the true effect; it makes its estimate unbiased.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 137</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Causality and Experimentation for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-137","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-37","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>When analyzing a daily defect-rate series, the FIRST check to make before applying classical tests is:</p>","opts":["<p>Whether there's autocorrelation, trend, or seasonality, because independence is an assumption of the classical tests.</p>","<p>Whether the number of observations is a multiple of 7.</p>","<p>Whether the data was collected by the same operator.</p>","<p>Whether the mean is greater than the median.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Time-series data frequently violates independence. Positive autocorrelation underestimates standard errors and produces spurious significance; trend and seasonality create associations between variables with no causal relationship at all (spurious regression). Checking the ACF/PACF is the first step, not the last.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The number of observations doesn't need any relationship to the weekly period.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It could be a relevant source of variation, but it isn't the primary check in a time-series analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Comparing mean and median indicates skew, a secondary issue relative to temporal dependence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 55</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Time Series in Quality: Autocorrelation, Trend, and Forecasting","chapterRef":"cap-055","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-38","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>A well-built Ishikawa diagram should end with:</p>","opts":["<p>The root cause, identified and confirmed.</p>","<p>A set of candidate causes to test with data, prioritized for verification.</p>","<p>The solution, approved by leadership.</p>","<p>The revised control plan.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The Ishikawa is a hypothesis-GENERATION tool, not a confirmation one. Its output is a prioritized list of suspects. Treating the most-voted cause from the session as a confirmed root cause is the mistake that makes teams implement solutions for problems that don't exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Confirmation requires data: a root-cause verification matrix, hypothesis testing, or an experiment.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The solution belongs to the Improve phase, and only after the cause has been proven.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The control plan is a Control deliverable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 36</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analyze","chapterRef":"cap-036","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-39","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Defect counts per panel show a variance much larger than the mean (ratio ≈ 4). The appropriate model is:</p>","opts":["<p>Exponential, since these are events.</p>","<p>Poisson, because the data is counts.</p>","<p>Negative binomial (or overdispersed Poisson), because Poisson assumes variance equal to the mean.</p>","<p>Normal, by the Central Limit Theorem.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Overdispersion signals unmodeled heterogeneity (panels, lots, or shifts with different propensities). Ignoring it and using Poisson produces underestimated standard errors and confidence intervals that are too narrow — apparent significance where there is none. On a control chart, the effect is excess false alarms on a c or u chart.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The exponential models the TIME BETWEEN events, not the count of events per unit.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Poisson requires Var = μ. With a ratio of 4, that assumption is clearly violated.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The CLT applies to means; it doesn't license treating overdispersed individual counts as normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 53</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Count Data: Poisson, Negative Binomial, and the Generalized Linear Model","chapterRef":"cap-053","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-40","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Six strongly correlated process variables need to be monitored together. What limitation do separate univariate charts have?</p>","opts":["<p>They can't be applied to more than three variables.</p>","<p>They require subgroups of size 1.</p>","<p>They can't detect shifts in the mean.</p>","<p>They ignore the correlation structure and the overall false-alarm rate: an anomalous combination of individually normal values goes unnoticed.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Two normally correlated variables can both sit within their individual limits and still form a combination that's impossible for the process — the joint control ellipse is considerably narrower than the rectangle formed by the separate charts. Hotelling's T² captures that information; six separate charts don't.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — There's no technical limit on the number of univariate charts; the problem is statistical, not computational.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Univariate charts work with any subgroup size.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Univariate charts do detect shifts in their own variable; what they miss is the joint information.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 56</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Multivariate Analysis: PCA and Hotelling's T² Control","chapterRef":"cap-056","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-41","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a causal DAG, conditioning on (controlling for) a COLLIDER variable — a common descendant of two variables — produces:</p>","opts":["<p>The introduction of spurious association between variables that were independent.</p>","<p>No effect, because colliders are irrelevant.</p>","<p>An automatic reduction in the estimator's variance.</p>","<p>Removal of confounding and a more precise estimate.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>It's counterintuitive, and that's what makes it dangerous: adjusting for a collider OPENS a non-causal path and creates correlation where there was none. That's why 'control for everything you measured' isn't the conservative move — it can be exactly what ruins the analysis. The DAG is the tool that distinguishes a confounder from a collider.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They're highly relevant: adjusting for them is one of the most common causes of wrong causal conclusions.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The effect is on bias, not on variance — and the bias introduced is systematic.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Removing confounding comes from adjusting for CONFOUNDERS (common causes), not for colliders.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 137</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Causality and Experimentation for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-137","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-42","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>The essential advantage of a factorial experiment over varying one factor at a time (OFAT) is that it:</p>","opts":["<p>Always requires fewer runs overall.</p>","<p>Estimates INTERACTIONS between factors, which OFAT is structurally unable to detect, and uses every run to estimate all the effects.</p>","<p>Dispenses with randomization.</p>","<p>Doesn't require replicates.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>In OFAT, each factor is evaluated with the others held at an arbitrary level — if A's effect depends on B's level, the OFAT result is only valid at that level of B, and no one finds out. The factorial estimates A's effect at both levels of B, and the difference between them is the interaction. It's also more efficient: every run contributes to every effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's often more efficient, but that isn't the essential advantage — and a full factorial with many factors can require more runs than a shallow OFAT.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Randomization remains essential in any experiment, to protect against uncontrolled factors.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Replicates are still needed to estimate experimental error (or center points are used for that purpose).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 73</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design of Experiments","chapterRef":"cap-073","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-43","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A 2³ factorial with center points shows the center points sitting significantly above the fitted plane. This indicates:</p>","opts":["<p>The need to increase the number of factors.</p>","<p>A measurement error at the center points.</p>","<p>Curvature: the linear model is inadequate, and you should move to response-surface methodology (CCD or Box-Behnken).</p>","<p>A significant three-factor interaction.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Center points are the factorial's cheap insurance: they compare the observed average at the center with the average predicted by the linear model. A significant difference is the formal lack-of-fit test for curvature — and it signals that an interior optimum exists that the linear model can't locate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Curvature is about the SHAPE of the response over the existing factors, not about missing factors.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Replicates at the center points are exactly what let you estimate pure error and distinguish noise from curvature.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The three-way interaction is estimable in the full factorial and is a distinct effect from quadratic curvature.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 77</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Response Surface Methodology: CCD, Box-Behnken, and Optimization","chapterRef":"cap-077","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-44","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In Taguchi robust design, the distinction between CONTROL factors and NOISE factors serves to:</p>","opts":["<p>Rank the factors by statistical importance.</p>","<p>Replace the need for statistical process control.</p>","<p>Reduce the experiment's cost by eliminating factors.</p>","<p>Find levels of the control factors that make the response INSENSITIVE to variation in the noise factors, instead of trying to eliminate the noise.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>It's a shift in economic strategy: instead of spending money to control ambient temperature, humidity, or supplier variation (expensive or impossible), you look for the combination of parameters where those variations stop mattering. It exploits the control × noise interaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Statistical importance is a result of the analysis; the control/noise classification is made beforehand, based on the factor's physical nature.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Robustness reduces sensitivity to noise, but the process still needs monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The inner/outer array design tends to increase the number of runs, not reduce it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 79</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Taguchi, Robust Design, and Tolerance Design","chapterRef":"cap-079","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-45","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>Before fully implementing a solution, the pilot serves mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Check on a reduced scale whether the expected effect materializes and surface unforeseen consequences, before committing full resources.</p>","<p>Convince leadership to approve the budget.</p>","<p>Train the operators on the new solution.</p>","<p>Satisfy a bureaucratic requirement of the methodology.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The rationale is risk management: fail fast and cheap. The pilot needs a success criterion defined BEFOREHAND, a measurement comparable to the baseline, a duration long enough to capture normal variation, and a rollback plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It can help with approval, but the goal is testing the solution hypothesis, not persuasion.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Training is a secondary benefit; the goal is validating the solution.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The pilot has substantive technical value; treating it as a formality wastes it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-46","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In a solution-selection matrix, the weighting of the criteria should be defined:</p>","opts":["<p>After seeing the scores, so the result is coherent.</p>","<p>Before scoring the alternatives, to prevent the weights from being adjusted to favor the preferred solution.</p>","<p>By the team's most experienced member, individually.</p>","<p>Equal for every criterion, always.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Setting weights after seeing the scores allows (even without bad intent) rationalizing a choice already made. Fixing the criteria and weights beforehand, and documenting them, is what makes the decision auditable and defensible.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That's exactly the sequence that compromises the tool's objectivity.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Individual weighting loses the cross-functional information and reduces the team's buy-in to the result.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Equal weights are a legitimate special case, but rarely reflect reality: safety and cost rarely carry the same weight.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-47","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A problem is characterized by long wait times between steps, with stable quality and adequate capability. The indicated improvement track is:</p>","opts":["<p>Failure reduction, with reliability analysis.</p>","<p>Variability reduction, with DOE and SPC.</p>","<p>Waste reduction, with Lean flow tools (VSM, kanban, leveling).</p>","<p>Defect reduction, with FMEA and poka-yoke.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Choosing the track before the tools avoids the 'generic DMAIC with every possible tool.' Waiting between steps with stable quality is a FLOW problem, not one of variation or defects — attacking it with DOE would burn months without touching the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Reliability deals with equipment or product failures over time, which isn't what's described.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — There's no evidence of excessive variability; capability is described as adequate.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There's no evidence of defects; the problem described is about time.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 41</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Beyond Generic DMAIC: the Four Continuous Improvement Paths","chapterRef":"cap-041","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-48","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>The CONTROL phase is only complete when:</p>","opts":["<p>The team is disbanded and celebrates the closeout.</p>","<p>The solution has been implemented across the whole plant.</p>","<p>The final report is approved by the Master Black Belt.</p>","<p>The control plan is in use, the process owner has formally accepted the handoff, and the gain has been verified after a period of stable operation.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The criterion is demonstrated sustainability, not implementation. Without a formal handoff to the process owner, the process regresses as soon as the project team disperses — the most well-documented failure pattern in Six Sigma programs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Celebrating is good recognition practice, not a completion criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Broad implementation doesn't guarantee the gain holds over time.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Document approval is necessary, but it doesn't demonstrate the gain persists.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-49","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>To quickly detect a small, sustained shift in the mean (about 0.5σ), the most suitable chart is:</p>","opts":["<p>An EWMA or CUSUM chart, which accumulate information across several observations.</p>","<p>A p chart.</p>","<p>A histogram updated weekly.</p>","<p>A Shewhart X̄ chart with 3σ limits.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The Shewhart chart evaluates each point in isolation and has a very long ARL for small shifts (hundreds of samples for 0.5σ). EWMA and CUSUM accumulate information over time and dramatically reduce that ARL — at the cost of responding more slowly to large shifts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A p chart is meant for proportions of defectives, not for shifts in the mean of a continuous variable.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A histogram has no time ordering and doesn't detect shifts — it would just show a widened distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It's the worst-performing chart precisely in this situation; it's excellent for large shifts (≥2σ).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 69</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"EWMA and CUSUM: Detecting Small Shifts","chapterRef":"cap-069","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-50","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>Six months after closure, the process indicator has regressed to the baseline level. The most likely cause is:</p>","opts":["<p>The original statistical analysis was wrong.</p>","<p>Control wasn't sustained: no layered audit, no training for new operators, or no defined reaction to the alarm.</p>","<p>The Hawthorne effect explains the entire original gain.</p>","<p>The target was too ambitious.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Regression to baseline is almost always a sustainment failure, not an analysis one: the procedure never got built into the standard work, new operators never got trained, the reaction plan was never triggered, the indicator stopped being tracked. Layered audits exist precisely to catch that erosion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Possible, but it would have been caught much earlier — in the pilot or the initial verification of the gain.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The Hawthorne effect can inflate short-term gains, but it rarely explains a gain verified and sustained over months.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — An ambitious target would produce a partial gain, not a complete reversal to the starting point.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-51","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>The essential difference between a control chart and a trend chart with targets is that the control chart:</p>","opts":["<p>Requires dedicated statistical software.</p>","<p>Uses different colors for the points.</p>","<p>Has limits derived from the PROCESS'S OWN VARIATION, letting you distinguish common cause from special cause and avoid reacting to noise.</p>","<p>Is updated more frequently.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Control limits ≠ specification limits ≠ targets. Control limits say what the process does; the specification says what the customer wants. Reacting to every point below target, in a stable process, is tampering: it increases variation instead of reducing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A control chart can be kept on paper; the math is elementary.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Visual formatting isn't the methodological difference.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Update frequency is irrelevant to the nature of the tool.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-57","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>For a characteristic classified as 'special' (high risk) on the control plan, the verification frequency should typically be:</p>","opts":["<p>Higher than for normal characteristics, with 100% inspection or reinforced control until capability is demonstrated and stable.</p>","<p>The same as the other characteristics, to keep the plan simple.</p>","<p>Lower, because high-risk characteristics were already validated during the design phase.</p>","<p>Decided exclusively by the customer, never by quality engineering.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The control plan should be proportional to risk: special characteristics get reinforced verification — 100% inspection, tighter control charts, or more frequent sampling — until there's statistical evidence of stability and capability. Reducing that control is only defensible once that evidence exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Treating every characteristic the same way ignores exactly what the risk classification is meant to communicate.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Design-stage validation (DFMEA, prototype) doesn't replace capability evidence from actual production, under real conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The customer can impose requirements, but the technical decision on control frequency is quality engineering's responsibility, backed by data.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 65</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Product and Process Control Methods","chapterRef":"cap-065","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-58","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>An Out-of-Control Action Plan (OCAP), tied to a control chart, should specify:</p>","opts":["<p>Only the chart's statistical control limits.</p>","<p>The history of every point already logged on the chart.</p>","<p>The immediate containment action, who has authority to decide it, and the criterion for escalating if the cause isn't identified quickly.</p>","<p>The shift's production target.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>An alarm with no defined OCAP creates hesitation: nobody knows whether to stop the line, segregate the lot, or wait. The OCAP turns the statistical signal into a concrete operational action — immediate containment, a decision owner, and a deadline/criterion for escalation if the cause isn't obvious.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The limits are the chart's statistical parameters, not a response action.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The point history is input data for diagnosis, not the reaction protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The production target is a business indicator, not part of the response to a statistical alarm.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-59","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>Unlike a Shewhart control chart, the limits of a pre-control chart:</p>","opts":["<p>Are calculated from σ estimated within the subgroups, just like the X̄ chart.</p>","<p>Are always wider than Shewhart's 3σ limits.</p>","<p>Can only be used on processes with Cpk above 2.0.</p>","<p>Derive from the product's SPECIFICATION — divided into green, yellow, and red zones — rather than from the process's observed variation.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Pre-control divides the tolerance (not the process variation) into zones: green in the center (half the tolerance), yellow at the edges, red outside the specification. That's the structural difference from Shewhart, whose limits always come from the process's actual variation (σ within subgroups), never from the specification.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's exactly the opposite: the X̄ chart uses the process's σ; pre-control uses the tolerance.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — There's no fixed width relationship between the two; it depends on how tight the tolerance is relative to the actual variation.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There's no universal Cpk threshold that authorizes or forbids using pre-control, though it works best with good capability.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze"},{"id":"CSSBB-52","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>DMADV (DFSS) should be chosen over DMAIC when:</p>","opts":["<p>The team doesn't have enough historical data.</p>","<p>The project has to be completed in under three months.</p>","<p>The process exists but performance falls short of what's required and the cause is unknown.</p>","<p>The product or process doesn't exist, or incremental improvement of the existing one can't reach the requirement.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>DMAIC improves what exists; DMADV creates what doesn't exist or replaces what's structurally incapable. The decisive signal is the performance ceiling: if even with the process perfectly centered and in control the requirement isn't achievable, a new design is needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A lack of historical data is solved with a collection plan in the Measure phase, not by switching methodologies.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A short deadline favors DMAIC; DMADV is typically longer.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That's exactly the situation DMAIC is for.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 33</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Introduction to DMAIC and DMADV","chapterRef":"cap-033","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-53","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>The concept of 'design for manufacturability' (DFM) translates, in practice, into:</p>","opts":["<p>Designing with the real capability of the available processes in mind, reducing part count, variants, and critical operations.</p>","<p>Automating the entire assembly line.</p>","<p>Widening the tolerances on every dimension.</p>","<p>Reducing the product's material cost.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Roughly 70-80% of a product's cost gets locked in at the design stage. Designing a ±0.01 mm tolerance for a process with an achievable Cpk of 0.8 at that tolerance creates a permanent quality problem — one that no downstream SPC fixes. DFM brings capability to the design table.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Automating is a process choice, not a design principle.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Indiscriminately widening tolerances compromises function; DFM seeks to align functional tolerance with real capability.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Material cost is only part of the cost; DFM spans assembly, tooling, inspection, and variants.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 95</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design Inputs, Techniques, and Review","chapterRef":"cap-095","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-54","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>In a tolerance analysis during the design phase, the metric that links the design decision to future production performance is:</p>","opts":["<p>The estimated unit cost.</p>","<p>The predicted capability (expected Cpk) of each critical characteristic, against the proposed tolerance and the process's known variation.</p>","<p>The development timeline.</p>","<p>The number of parts in the assembly.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Assigning tolerances without confronting the process's real variation is writing a quality problem into the drawing. The predicted Cpk per characteristic is what lets you negotiate between function, cost, and feasibility BEFORE the tooling is built.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cost is a decision criterion, but it doesn't predict conformance.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Timeline is a project constraint, not a product-performance indicator.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Part count affects tolerance stacking, but it isn't the linking metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 113</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma in Healthcare","chapterRef":"cap-113","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"},{"id":"CSSBB-55","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>When designing an AI-based automated visual inspection system, the requirement quality engineering must impose from the start is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Ensure the model is fully autonomous, with no human oversight.</p>","<p>Maximize the model's overall accuracy.</p>","<p>Define the asymmetric cost of the errors (false accept vs. false reject), the corresponding decision threshold, and the revalidation and drift-monitoring plan.</p>","<p>Use the most recent neural-network architecture available.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In an inspection, letting a bad part through and rejecting a good one have radically different costs — and overall accuracy treats them as equivalent. The threshold should be derived from that cost. And since the model degrades with changes in lighting, lot, or supplier, revalidation and drift monitoring are requirements, not extras.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Human oversight is exactly one of the controls required by ISO/IEC 42001 and ISO/IEC 23894 for high-consequence decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — With imbalanced classes (few bad parts), a model that approves everything reaches very high accuracy and is useless.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Architecture is a secondary technical choice relative to correctly framing the decision problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 112</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"AI in Quality and Its Governance: ISO/IEC 42001 and ISO/IEC 23894","chapterRef":"cap-112","cognitiveLevel":"Apply"},{"id":"CSSBB-56","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>A well-CALIBRATED prediction model means that:</p>","opts":["<p>It never gets the predicted class wrong.</p>","<p>It was trained on the largest available data set.</p>","<p>It has the highest possible AUC among the candidate models.</p>","<p>Among the cases it assigns a probability of 0.30, approximately 30% actually occur.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Calibration is the correspondence between predicted probability and observed frequency. A model can rank perfectly (high AUC) and be poorly calibrated, predicting 0.9 where the real frequency is 0.5. When the probability feeds a cost-based decision — inspect or not, hold or release — calibration matters more than ranking.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — No useful model is infallible; calibration is about the reliability of the probabilities, not the absence of error.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Data volume doesn't guarantee calibration, especially if there's drift between training and production.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — AUC measures RANKING ability (discrimination) and is invariant to monotonic transformations of the probability — so it's indifferent to calibration.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 138</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Model Validation, Calibration, Drift, and Model Risk","chapterRef":"cap-138","cognitiveLevel":"Understand"}]);
